1 NAME Hugh "An Shenilir Mor" /Mackay/ 3rd of Scoury
2 GIVN Hugh "An Shenilir Mor"
2 SURN Mackay
2 NSFX 3rd of Scoury
2 _MARNM Mackay
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1640
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Scotland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 3 AUG 1692
2 PLAC Steenkerque
2 ADDR
3 STAE Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
3 CTRY (present-day Belgium)
1 TITL General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} III. General Hugh Mackay of Scoury, known among his countrymen as An Shenilir Mor, The Great General, to distinguish him from other generals of the name of Mackay. In our memoir of the 3rd Lord Reay we gave an acco
2 CONC unt of his life to which we now refer the reader. He m. at Bommel in Guelderland Clara de Bie, a Dutch lady of noble extraction, and had issue a son and three daus:--—
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh, of whom follows. The daus. were :—
2 CONT * Margaret, m. George, 3rd Lord Reay, withissue ; 
2 CONT * Anna Barbara, m. Mr. Reyard, minister of Nimeguen
2 CONT * and Mary, m. Mathew Linyindeck, burgomaster of Nimeguen.
2 CONT
2 CONT pg. 290 
2 CONT
2 CONT THE BOOK OF MACKAY BY ANGUS MACKAY, M.A. (St. Andrews University)
2 CONT  Minister at Westerdale, Caithness EDINBURGH : NORMAN MACLEOD,  -25 GEORGE IV. BRIDGE CANADA : EDWARD MACKAY, ST. PETER'S MANSE,
2 CONT  MADOC, ONT. M DCCCC VI
2 CONT
2 CONT  ===========================
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Mackay
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Mackay (c. 1640 – 3 August 1692) was a Scottish general best known for his service in the Revolution of 1688.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early military career
2 CONT
2 CONT Mackay was born in Sutherland around 1640, the son of Hugh Mackay of Scourie. He entered Douglas's (Dumbarton's) regiment of the English army (now the Royal Scots) in 1660 and accompanied it to France when it was lent by Charles I
2 CONC I to Louis XIV. Although Mackay succeeded, through the death of his two elder brothers, to his father's estates, he continued to serve abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1669 he was in the Venetian service at Crete, and in 1672 he was back with his old regiment, Dumbartons, in the French army, taking part under Turenne in the invasion of Holland. In 1673 he married Clara de Bie of Bommel in Gel
2 CONC derland. Through her influence he became, as historian Gilbert Burnet wrote, "the most pious man that I ever knew in a military way." Convinced that he was fighting in an unjust cause, Mackay resigned his commission to take a capt
2 CONC aincy in a Scottish regiment in the Dutch service.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had risen to the rank of major-general in 1685, when the Scots brigade was called to England to assist in the suppression of the Monmouth rebellion. Returning to Holland, Mackay was one of those officers who elected to stay wit
2 CONC h their men when James II, having again demanded the services of the Scots brigade, and having been met with a refusal, was permitted to invite the officers individually into his service. As major-general commanding the brigade, a
2 CONC nd also as a privy councillor of Scotland, Mackay was an important and influential person, and James chose to attribute the decision of most of the officers to Mackay's instigation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolution of 1688 http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glorious_Revolution
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after this event, William, Prince of Orange, started on his expedition to England. Mackay's division led the invading corps. In January 1689 Mackay was appointed major-general commanding in chief in Scotland. In this capacity
2 CONC  he was called upon to deal with the formidable insurrection headed by John, Viscount Dundee. In the Battle of Killiecrankie, Mackay was severely defeated, but the Jacobite victory was nullified by the death of Dundee.  http://en.
2 CONC wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Killiecrankie
2 CONT
2 CONT Thereafter, Mackay, displaying unexpected energy, subdued the Highlands in one summer. In 1690 he founded Fort William at Inverlochy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1691 he distinguished himself in the brilliant victory at the Battle of Aughrim. http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Aughrim
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1692, with the rank of lieutenant-general, he commanded the British division of the allied army in Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Battle of Steinkeerke, Mackay's division bore the brunt of the day unsupported and the general himself was killed. http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Steenkerque
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Mackay and his wife had three children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hugh Mackay (1681–1708), married Anna Louise de Lannoy
2 CONT
2 CONT  Margaret Mackay (1683–1748), married George Mackay, 3rd Lord Reay
2 CONT
2 CONT  Maria Mackay (1686-1723?), married Matthias Lambertus Singendonck
2 CONT
2 CONT Cultural significance
2 CONT
2 CONT Mackay was the inventor of the socket bayonet which soon came into general use, the idea of this being suggested to him by the failure of the plug bayonet to stop the rush of the Highlanders at the Battle of Killiecrankie.
2 CONT
2 CONT He may be know to some people nowadays from a mention in the song Braes o' Killiecrankie: "O fie Macvkay what gart ye flie".
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mackay, John (1836). Life of Lieut. Gen. Hugh Mackay of Scourie. Edinburgh: Lang & Forbes.
2 CONT
2 CONT =======================
2 CONT
2 CONT THE SCOWRY BRANCH OF MACKAYS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Mackay, who was worthily termed the great general, [see p. 484), to whom the succession belonged, married Clara de Bie of Bommelin Guelderland, by  whom he had son, 
2 CONT * Hugh,  and three daughters
2 CONT * Margaret, 
2 CONT >Margaret, the eldest daughter of General Hugh of Scowry, married George, third Lord Reay
2 CONT * Anna-Barbara, and 
2 CONT >his second daughter, Anna-Barbara, married Mr Reyerd, minister of Numeguen
2 CONT * Mary. 
2 CONT >Mary, married Matthew Linyindeck, burgomaster of Numeguen: both of whom died without issue
2 CONT  	 Pg. 562-3  HISTORY THE HOUSE AND CLAN MACKAY,BY ROBERT MACKAY, 1829
