1 NOTE In a document dated 1706, one finds that Knut Nilsson Finne was the verger for the churches in Voss parish.  In the same document it states that there was a big upheaval regarding this.  A lot of the woodwork had to be repaired.  
2 CONC He obtained the timber from a 'a side valley in Raundalen three and a half miles away'.  Then he built a sawmill and did the required work.  It was also Knut who installed the pews in the church about 1720. Knut was a discriminati
2 CONC ng and skillful artisan and a valuable man in the district.  His wife, Margreta Miltzow, "Bailiff's daughter", was an authoritative female, one who knew what she was worth and where she was from.  Thus it was told that after the '
2 CONC flap' about the church pews, and each farm had been assigned its pew (some farms shared the same pew) and the Finne pew was in the tenth row, Margreta didn't think twice about it. The first time she went to church after the repair
2 CONC s were completed she went directly to the fourth pew and sat down.  "My husband installed these pews and I'll sit wherever I want to", she is quoted to have said. Thus it came to pass that the Finne pew was the fourth in line. Mar
2 CONC greta was not timid.  She represented her husband in court against several claimants who had a couple of major cases against him.  In those times it is told, there was once such gale weather that there was ice way out in the fjord
2 CONC s so that one could drive all the way to Bergen. During this storm Margreta took a trip to town and wore out two horses in the process.  Thus say the legends.     A little poem about Margreta that has survived by word of mouth unt
2 CONC il this day: I came to the wedding farm,     I met the bridegroom's father;     He invited me in.     When I was inside     There sat Knut Finne Drinking with his guests.     She, Margreta Finne,     took off in a hast to get cake
2 CONC  and lefse.     She told me to eat;     Then she wanted to know     All the gossip around. Legend has it that it was a traveling hobo that coined these lines. In such a way he wanted to describe what had happened to him on a trip 
2 CONC through Finne at wedding time.
