1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Dirck Jansz Graeff=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Jan Pietersz Graeff and Stijntge Jansdochter Braseman
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.dbnl.org/tekst/molh003nieu02_01/molh003nieu02_01_1038.php New Dutch biographical dictionary. Part 2 (1912)]
2 CONT
2 CONT GRAEFF (Dirck Jansz. de ), 1529 - 25 July 1589, was the third and best-known son of Jan Pietersz. (col. 501). He also seems to have run the iron trade in his father's house 'de Keyser' on the Damrak. He was first married to Agniet
2 CONC  Pietersdr. van Neck since 1557. Well-known among the leading Reformed Christians, he fled the city with his wife and children in 1568 and went to Embden. However, he does not appear on the list of 'fugitives'. Before his flight h
2 CONC e liquidated his entire fortune, took interest on his house from his brother Jacob, among other things, and sent all his money, a sum of 60,000 gl., in two iron chests to his friend Jan Jacobsz. Huydecoper, who had already settled
2 CONC  in Embden. In 1576 we find him with his wife in Utrecht, where both make their wills before the notary Dirck Jansz. van Woerden. In September thereafter his wife died in Embden. Already in December of the same year he is again in
2 CONC  Utrecht, where he and Pieter Bicker, in place of Hendrik van Comhuyzen, receive a commission from the General States, to conclude a loan for a sum of 600,000 gl. in the Netherlands, in Oostland, Hamburg and Bremen. Not much later
2 CONC  he seems to have settled in Hoorn, but at the change of government in May 1578 he is in Amsterdam, becomes a member of the city council and soon mayor. In the summer of 1580 he settled in Haarlem, but returned the following year,
2 CONC  but withdrew from all government services and obtained a decision or act of promise from the city council, that he would remain outside the election. He seems to have remained a member of the city council in the meantime, had bee
2 CONC n a prominent shipowner since then and was interested in the navigation of more than 100 ships, mainly traded in salt to Portugal and is the highest taxed in the register of the personal quotisation of 1584. He bought, among other
2 CONC  things, a lot of land in the vicinity of Voorschoten and in 1581 also the Vredenhof farm there, largely destroyed by the Spaniards; the reconstruction, which he started, was completed by his son Jan Dirksz. As regent of the Burge
2 CONC rweeshuis, he gave a 'publicque maeltijd' on the occasion of the marriage of his daughter Weyntje with Jacob Boelens , on 10 Jan. 1584, both to the orphans and to the patients in the Dolhuis, Old men's and women's hospice and to t
2 CONC hose of the Leper House. He remarried shortly afterwards with Teuwt Jansdr. Cat , with whom he left no children. He died in 1589 and was buried in the Oude Kerk on 25 July.
2 CONT
2 CONT His anonymously painted portrait, still preserved by his family, was engraved by J. Houbraken.
2 CONT Besides the aforementioned daughter Weyntje, three sons from his first marriage survived him: Jacob (col. 498); Jan Dirksz. Graeff , who died unmarried in 1627 at Vredenhof in the Hofland near Voorschoten; and Pieter Dirksz. Graef
2 CONC f 1573-1645, who in 1613 made a journey through Palestine and visited many holy places there. In 1620 he bought the lordship of Engelenburg under Herwijnen. He lived on the Fluweelenburgwal and died unmarried. See: Literature in P
2 CONC ieter de Graeff (col. 502 ff.). Feather
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diederik_Jansz._Graeff Diederik Jansz Graeff, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Diederik Jansz Graeff, also Dirk Jansz Graeff, Lord of the manor Vredenhof (Amsterdam 1532 - Jul 27 1589), first illustrious member of the De Graeff family, was a rich merchant, Ship-owner and Politician. Diederik Graeff was also 
2 CONC the founder of a regent dynastie of the Dutch Golden Age and the short time of the First Stadtholderless Period that retained power and influence for centuries and produced a number of ministers. He was the first Mayor of Amsterda
2 CONC m from the De Graeff family.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
